WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.360
Why is a cell the size it is?

2
00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.340
Start with one reason.

3
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.680
Everything a cell takes in crosses its surface.

4
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.460
Everything it uses is spread through its volume.

5
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.640
So what happens as a cell grows?

6
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.153
Take a hypothetical smooth spherical cell.

7
00:00:24.593 --> 00:00:28.273
Let its radius grow from 5 micrometres to 10.

8
00:00:29.093 --> 00:00:30.993
The area rises fourfold.

9
00:00:31.693 --> 00:00:33.813
The volume rises eightfold.

10
00:00:34.873 --> 00:00:36.973
Area is 4 pi r squared.

11
00:00:37.853 --> 00:00:40.593
Volume is 4 pi r cubed over 3.

12
00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:46.993
For a sphere, the ratio of area to volume is 3 divided by the radius.

13
00:00:46.993 --> 00:00:52.833
So the ratio halves, from 0.6 to 0.3 per micrometre.

14
00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:55.967
Now invent two rates.

15
00:00:56.567 --> 00:00:58.847
They are made-up inputs, not measurements.

16
00:00:59.847 --> 00:01:05.227
Say the membrane can take up a nutrient at no more than 20 molecules per square

17
00:01:05.227 --> 00:01:06.807
micrometre each second.

18
00:01:07.607 --> 00:01:13.267
Say the cell uses 6 molecules per cubic micrometre each second, evenly through its

19
00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:19.600
volume. Assume the whole surface takes up nutrient, and nothing else limits the cell.

20
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.280
Now compare supply with demand.

21
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.980
At 5 micrometres, supply is 6,283 molecules per second.

22
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:35.200
Demand is 3,142. So supply is twice demand.

23
00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.860
At 10 micrometres, supply only just meets demand.

24
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.340
The ratio is exactly 1.

25
00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:47.440
Supply keeps up to a radius of 3 times 20 divided by 6.

26
00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.640
That is 10 micrometres.

27
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.210
The arithmetic leaves things out.

28
00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:57.170
Change the rates or the shape, and the answer changes.

29
00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:03.570
A flat or long thin cell keeps much more surface per volume than a sphere.

30
00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.250
A plant vacuole can fill most of the cell.

31
00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:14.790
A root epidermal cell adds volume mostly as vacuole, so its cytoplasm grows far less

32
00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:15.790
than the cell does.

33
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.750
In Arabidopsis root epidermis, cells grew about 14-fold in volume.

34
00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:27.370
The space between vacuole and cell boundary grew only about two- to threefold.

35
00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.100
Keep the rule. Supply comes in through the surface.

36
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.720
Demand is spread through the volume.

37
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:40.160
As the radius grows, the ratio of capacity to demand falls.

38
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.100
In this model it is 10 divided by the radius.

39
00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:48.660
That is a scaling penalty, not a universal maximum.
